@h1 Creative writing without indentation h1@

Write fiction where the source reads like the finished page — flat paragraphs, no
indentation, scenes and emphasis in the lightest possible markup. The file below
is complete and builds green.

@h2 The whole story h2@

@@@pre
@h2 One · The Harbour h2@

The rain had not stopped for three days.

Mara pressed her palm to the cold glass and counted the boats that were
no longer there. _One. Two._ The third had been her father's.

#scenebreak: ✦ ✦ ✦ !!

@scenebreak

"You can't stay," the harbourmaster said.

She said nothing. There was *nothing*, she had learned, that saying could fix.

@hr hr@

And then the tide turned.
pre@@@

@h2 What each piece does h2@

@ul
@li @strong Paragraphs. strong@ Just type them, a blank line between. No leading
spaces, no wrapping node — each block becomes its own paragraph. li@
@li @strong Emphasis. strong@ @code _One. Two._ code@ becomes italics;
@code *nothing* code@ becomes bold. The marks wrap a run of text and nothing
else. li@
@li @strong A scene heading. strong@ @strong h2 strong@ opens the chapter. li@
@li @strong A scene break. strong@ Define an ornament once —
@@@code #scenebreak: ✦ ✦ ✦ !! code@@@ — and drop it in as a bare
@@@code @scenebreak code@@@. For a plain rule, @@@code @hr hr@ code@@@ is core
vocabulary. li@
ul@

@h2 Build h2@

@@@pre
wit build story.wit --fragment
pre@@@

Or @code -o story.pdf code@ for print. Verified: each block is its own
paragraph; @code _One. Two._ code@ renders as italics and @code *nothing* code@
as bold; @@@code @scenebreak code@@@ prints the ornament; @@@code @hr hr@ code@@@
renders a horizontal rule. The apostrophe in @code father's code@ passes through
correctly.

@h2 Pitfalls h2@

@ul
@li @strong There is no @code break code@ node. strong@ Use @@@code @hr hr@ code@@@
or a definition of your own for a divider — @code break code@ is not core
vocabulary. li@
@li @strong Nest emphasis outer-italic. strong@ @@@code _*x*_ code@@@ leaves the
inner @code * code@ literal; write @@@code *_x_* code@@@ to get bold italics. li@
ul@

@h2 Variations h2@

Name an epigraph as a reusable value definition and recall it with a bare
reference; give a scene heading a subtitle with an interpuncted @strong h2 strong@
title, as above.

